    <section class="content-section">
      <div class="container grid-2">
        <div>
          <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">Democratizing Knowledge &amp; Action</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.2rem; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem;">
            True development has never been about grand monuments or glossy economic statistics. Decades ago, four visionaries—<strong>C. Achutha Menon</strong>, <strong>Dr. K.N. Raj</strong>, <strong>Laurie Baker</strong>, and <strong>T.R. Chandradutt ("Dutt Mash")</strong>—came together with a shared conviction: that planning, habitat, and science belong in the hands of ordinary people[cite: 1].
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 1.2rem; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Through pioneering efforts that laid the ground for COSTFORD and, later, the Laurie Baker Centre for Habitat Studies, they demystified architecture and economic policy[cite: 1]. They proved that human dignity, ecological sense, and decentralized democracy build far more resilient communities than top-down mandates[cite: 1].
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem;">
            People &amp; Development has inherited that spirit of fearless questioning[cite: 1]. Today that spirit is not an excursion into nostalgia for us, but our very orientation[cite: 1]. The realities confronting us in the present century—erratic climate patterns, precarious labour, an aging society, and unbridled urbanization—demand the same plain-spoken courage[cite: 1].
          </p>
        </div>

